      "math_academy_id": "q-122599",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function",
      "problem": "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y).$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
