    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122829",
      "knowledge_point_id": "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot",
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109119/assets/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109119/assets/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109119/assets/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)",
